Objective
Build a production-ready Tic-Tac-Toe mobile application that demonstrates clean architecture, strong UX discipline, and scalable engineering practices. This is not a toy demo. Treat it as a portfolio-quality product.

Target Platforms

iOS and Android

Single shared codebase preferred (Flutter or React Native). Native is acceptable only if justified.

Core Functional Requirements

Classic 3×3 Tic-Tac-Toe gameplay

Two modes:

Player vs Player (local)

Player vs AI

AI must support at least two difficulty levels:

Easy: random or semi-random logic

Hard: unbeatable (Minimax or equivalent algorithm)

Clear win, draw, and invalid move detection

Game reset and replay without app restart

Non-Functional Requirements (This Is Where Most Apps Fail)

Instant response time (<100ms for AI move)

No unnecessary animations or gimmicks

Offline-first, zero network dependency

Deterministic game logic with full test coverage

Architecture Expectations

Separation of concerns is mandatory (UI, game logic, state management)

State management must be explicit and predictable (no spaghetti state)

Business logic must be framework-agnostic

Code must be readable by another senior engineer without explanation

UX / UI Standards

Clean, minimal design

Touch targets must meet accessibility guidelines

Clear visual feedback for:

Current player

Winning line

Game end state

No clutter. If an element does not add value, remove it.

Testing & Quality Bar

Unit tests for:

Win logic

Draw logic

AI decision making

No magic numbers

No hard-coded UI logic tied to game rules

Deliverables

Fully working mobile app

Well-structured source code

README explaining:

Architecture decisions

AI approach

Trade-offs made and why

Success Criteria
The app must be boring in the best possible way: predictable, fast, correct, and clean.
If it feels like a coding exercise, it has failed.
If it feels like a finished product, it has passed.